WHAT THE SCORE MEANS
The score is the effort the candidate needs to start interviewing for this job right now. The less preparation is needed, the higher the score. Preparation means preparing for the INTERVIEW: reading documentation, refreshing topics, practicing typical questions. It does not mean becoming a perfect employee.

PREPARATION BUCKETS AND THEIR SCORE RANGES
- ready: nothing to prepare. Score 10.
- days: a couple of days of preparation. Score 8-9.
- weeks: one to two weeks. Score 7.
- month: about a month. Score 5-6.
- months: several months of real practice. Score 2-4.
- impossible: a different profession, a hopeless gap, or a blocker. Score 0-1.
The final score must lie inside the range of the chosen bucket.

TWO KINDS OF MISSING SKILLS
1. Learnable from documentation in days: another library or framework in a language the candidate already uses, another database or vector store, another LLM provider or its API, an API style, a cloud service, a CI tool, a testing tool, project tools, basic Docker, Terraform or Kubernetes as a user. Bucket: days. Several such gaps together (three or more) count as weeks.
2. Needs practice: the main programming language of the role, a different paradigm, a deep specialization, managing people, and any "N+ years of X" requirement where the candidate has none of X at all. Bucket: months. These are never learnable "in a couple of days".

PROGRAMMING LANGUAGES
The main language of the role is the language the candidate would write application code in day to day. If it is not in the resume or LinkedIn, the bucket is impossible (score 0-1). Exception: a language very close to one the candidate uses professionally (for example JavaScript and TypeScript, or Java and Kotlin), where the candidate could realistically get interview-ready in weeks. Then use the months bucket (score 2-4) at best. Never treat a new main language as "quick to pick up".

YEARS OF EXPERIENCE
Required years are a convention, not a literal number, because interview preparation covers specific topics. A gap of a year or two (asks 3+, candidate has 1-2) does not change the bucket. Asks several times more than the candidate has (asks 8, candidate has 2): impossible. Asks n years of something the candidate has never used: months.

ROLE CAPS
The role can only lower the score, never raise it. The cap applies on top of the bucket range.
- Role closeness: "same" (no cap): the posting's profession and focus match a role the candidate already held, judged by the resume's job titles and actual work. "adjacent" (cap 8): same profession, different focus. "other_field" (cap 6): the candidate could do it, but the resume shows no work like this. "other_profession" (cap 2): a different craft entirely.
- Level: "same" (no cap), "slightly_off" (cap 7), "far_off" (cap 2). Far off means, for example, an internship for students only, a people-manager role the candidate has never held, or an executive role far above the candidate's experience.

PROCEDURE
Follow the five stages in order. Every stage has a thinking tag for free-form reasoning and a result tag in a strict format. Keep every thinking tag to at most 4 short sentences. If any blocker is triggered in stage 1, still write the tags of stages 2-4 but with the shortest possible content, set the bucket to impossible and the score to 0.

STAGE 1 - BLOCKERS
Thinking: for each blocker, decide what the posting would have to say for it to apply. Search the FULL text, including later paragraphs that refine or contradict the header (a header may say "Hybrid" while the body says fully remote is possible). Separate two situations: the posting explicitly states something that conflicts with the blocker (triggered), and the posting says nothing about it (not triggered). Silence never triggers a blocker. A bare city name in the header is not evidence of on-site work. Explicit "on-site", "hybrid" or "in office" for a blocker that demands fully remote work is a conflict. If the posting offers alternatives ("degree OR equivalent experience") and the candidate fits one of them, the blocker is not triggered.
Result: one line per blocker in the format: blocker: <short name> | vacancy: <exact short quote or "not stated"> | result: triggered / not triggered

STAGE 2 - ROLE
Thinking: work out what the company is really hiring for and what this person would do day to day, ignoring company marketing. Compare that with the candidate's past titles and the real work described in the resume. Decide whether this is the same profession, a neighbouring one, or a different one. Judge the seniority the posting expects against the candidate's level.
Result: <hiring> one sentence on who is wanted and for what work </hiring>
<closeness> one of: same / adjacent / other_field / other_profession </closeness>
<level> one of: same / slightly_off / far_off </level>

STAGE 3 - REQUIREMENTS
Thinking: pick the requirements that are truly central to the job, ignoring generic company boilerplate and soft-skill wording. For each one, look for the closest evidence in the resume and LinkedIn; a similar tool or technology counts as close. For every requirement that is missing, decide honestly which kind of missing skill it is (documentation in days, or practice) and how long it would take to be interview-ready. Write at most 12 requirements, the most important first.
Result: one <req> tag per requirement in the format: requirement | candidate evidence or "none" | match: exact / close / days / weeks / month / months / impossible

STAGE 4 - PREPARATION
Thinking: the slowest central requirement decides the bucket. Three or more "days" gaps together make it "weeks". Check whether the main programming language is covered. Check the bucket against the role and level caps from stage 2, and remember the score is limited by whichever is lower.
Result: <bucket> one of: ready / days / weeks / month / months / impossible </bucket>

STAGE 5 - SCORE
Thinking: pick the exact number inside the bucket range, closer to the top when the gaps are minor and the role matches well, closer to the bottom when several gaps pile up. Apply any scoring factors. Apply the role and level caps. If a blocker was triggered, the score is 0.
Result: one <con> tag per con, one <pro> tag per pro, then <score>. Each <con> and <pro> holds exactly one plain-text sentence and no other tags inside.
- Cons: only real problems: any requirement with match weeks, month, months or impossible, a triggered blocker, a role or level mismatch. Never list a "days" gap. If there is nothing serious, write no cons.
- Pros: what matches well.
- Each bullet is one sentence, at most 15 words, at most 4 bullets per list.
- The score is the last thing you write: one integer from 0 to 10.

